#### Double counting

The \(D^{0}\) is reconstructed via hadronic decay channel: \(D^{0}\to K^{-}+\pi^{+}\). If \(K^{-}\) is mis-identified as a \(\pi^{-}\) and \(\pi^{+}\) is mis-identified as a \(K^{+}\), the \((K^{+}\pi^{-})\) combination will contribute in the \(\overline{D^{0}}\) reconstruction, and if it falls into the \(\overline{D^{0}}\) mass selection window, this \(D^{0}\) signal will be then also counted as a \(\overline{D^{0}}\) signal. This is called double counting.

For \(D^{*}\) reconstruction via \(D^{\pm}\to D^{0}\pi^{\pm}_{S}\to K^{\mp}\pi^{\pm}\pi^{\pm}_{S}\), the situation is different. If \(K^{\mp}\) is misidentified as \(\pi^{\mp}\) and \(\pi^{\pm}\) as \(K^{\pm}\), then the combination becomes \(K^{\pm}\pi^{\mp}\pi^{\pm}_{S}\) which doesn't contribute to the \(\Delta M\) peak, but into the Wrong-sign background. Hence the raw yield obtained by the Wrong-sign background subtraction is undercounted.

Once again, the Monte-carlo Toy model (section B.2) was employed to produce \(g^{\rm RC}(t,M^{\rm RC})\) (see section 4.4, particularly Figure 4.10). Furthermore, if both kaon and pion happened to be in the overlapping PID region, an additional histogram \(g^{\rm RC}(t,M^{\rm Mis})\) was filled. \(M^{\rm Mis}\) is the invariant mass of mis-identified kaon (actually a pion) and pion (actually a kaon). This so called mis-identified kaon/pion was simply created by replacement of the rest mass in the first particles's four-momentum with the second particles's rest mass and vice versa. The fraction of the over counting in the Wrong-sign background was calculated then as:

\[f_{\rm OC}(t)\equiv \frac{\int\limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}1.84\\ 1.89\\ 1.84\end{subarray}}^{1.89}g^{\rm RC}(t,M^{\rm Mis})\mathrm{d}M^{\rm Mis}}{\int \limits_{\begin{subarray}{c}1.89\\ 1.84\end{subarray}}^{1.89}g^{\rm RC}(t,M^{\rm RC})\mathrm{d}M^{\rm RC}} \tag{5.3}\]

The probabilities of a kaon and a pion in the overlapping PID region are shown in Figure 5.1, \(g^{\rm RC}(t,M^{\rm RC})\) and \(g^{\rm RC}(t,M^{\rm Mis})\) in Figure 5.2, and \(f_{\rm OC}(t)\) in Figure 5.3.

