## Chapter 6 Results

The reconstruction of \(D^{0}\) in p+p collisions at \(\sqrt{s}=200\) GeV (Run9 pp200 analysis) and reconstruction of \(D^{0}\) and \(D^{*\pm}\) in p+p collisions at \(\sqrt{s}=500\) GeV (Run11 pp500 analysis) at STAR via the hadronic decay channels

\[D^{*\pm}\xrightarrow[p^{*}=39\text{ MeV/c}]{B.R.=3.89\%}K^{\mp}\pi^{\pm}\]

\[D^{0}\xrightarrow[\text{$B.R.=3.89\%$}]{B.R.=3.89\%}K^{\mp}\pi^{\pm},\]

where \(B.R.\) are branching ratios of these open charm mesons to pions and kaons [4], were done. 51.8 million and 107.8 minimum bias events have been analyzed in Run11 pp500 and Run9 pp200 analysis respectively. Both are described in chapter 3. Detector efficiency and acceptance and other efficiencies and corrections were discussed in chapter 4. Systematic uncertainties were discussed in chapter 5.

This chapter presents final results of the both \(p_{T}\)-differential and \(p_{T}\)-integrated invariant cross section of charm-anticharm quark pair poduction at mid-rapidity measurements and total charm-anticharm quark pair cross section estimation using PYTHIA [32] simulation.

These results are compared to theoretical model calculations and physics implications are discussed.

The differential invariant cross section \(E\frac{\mathrm{d}^{3}\sigma}{\mathrm{d}p^{3}}\) of an charm quark pair \(c\bar{c}\) production at mid-rapidity was calculated according to equation

\[E\frac{\mathrm{d}^{3}\sigma}{\mathrm{d}p^{3}}\bigg{|}_{y=0}=\left.\frac{\mathrm{ d}^{2}\sigma}{2\pi p_{T}\mathrm{d}p_{T}\mathrm{d}y}\right|_{y=0}=\frac{1}{2} \frac{1}{2\pi}\frac{\sigma^{\mathrm{NSD}}\beta}{N\mathfrak{f}_{c}\Gamma}\frac {Y}{p_{T}\Delta p_{T}\Delta y}\frac{1}{\varepsilon}, \tag{6.1}\]

where \(\sigma^{\mathrm{NSD}}\) is inelastic non-singly diffractive p+p cross section, \(\beta\) is the trigger bias (section 4.5), \(N\) is the total number of events entered the analysis, \(\mathfrak{f}_{c}\) represents the ratio of a charm quark hadronizing to an open charm meson, \(\Gamma\) denotes the branching ratio of a decay, and \(Y\) is the raw yield in a \(p_{T}\) bin of the width \(\Delta p_{T}\) within the rapidity window \(\Delta y\). \(\varepsilon\) is the combined efficiency of the experiment including the track reconstruction efficiency \(\varepsilon_{\mathrm{R}}\) (see section 4.1), the track matching efficiency \(\varepsilon_{\mathrm{M}}\) (see section 4.2), the particle identification efficiency \(\varepsilon_{\mathrm{PID}}\) (see section 4.3), and the kinematical cuts efficiencies \(\varepsilon_{\mathrm{Mass}}\) and \(\varepsilon_{\theta}\) (see section 4.4). Results from Run9 pp500 analysis are summarized in Table 6.1 and results from Run11 pp500 analysis are summarized in Table 6.2. Both tables show values of all variables used in equation (6.1). Some variables don't depend on reconstructed \(D^{0}/D^{*}\)\(p_{T}\) so they are displayed in cells stretched over more columns. Rows between thick horizontal rules show variables and values which are then corrected on bin widths (the bin width correction is discussed further in the text) and plotted in Figures 6.4, 6.1, 6.2, and 6.3.

Values of \(\mathfrak{f}_{c}\) and \(\Gamma\) were taken from Ref. [4]. \(\sigma^{\mathrm{NSD}}\) was measured for p+p collisions at \(\sqrt{s}=200\) at STAR [77]. There is no such measurement at \(\sqrt{s}=500\) GeV, hence the measured value from p+p collisions at \(\sqrt{s}=200\) GeV was scaled by Pythia with assumption that the systematic error at \(\sqrt{s}=500\) GeV differs negligibly from the one at \(\sqrt{s}=200\) GeV.

