### A.2 As a Levy Function

As an alternative to Hagedorn (A.1) formula, one can use a different approach based on the Tsallis statistics [86] to fit particle spectra. The Tsallis distribution was derived from a generalized form of the Boltzmann-Gibbs entropy. However, there are other originsdiscussed in recent days [87] suggesting for example hard collisions approach [88]. The distribution could be written in the form:

\[\frac{\mathrm{d}^{2}\sigma}{2\pi p_{T}\mathrm{d}p_{T}\mathrm{d}y}=C_{n}\left(1+ \frac{\sqrt{p_{T}^{2}+m_{0}^{2}}}{nT}\right)^{-n}\] (A.3)

where \(C_{n}\) is the normalization constant, \(n\) the power and \(T\) an inverse slope parameter.

To calculate \(C_{n}\), one must integrate (A.3) over \(p_{T}\)

\[C_{n}=\frac{1}{\int_{0}^{\infty}p_{T}\left(1+\frac{\sqrt{p_{T}^{2}+m_{0}^{2}}} {nT}\right)^{-n}\mathrm{d}p_{T}}=\frac{1}{\frac{(nT+m)^{1-n}}{(nT)^{-n}}\frac{ m(n-1)+nT}{(n-1)(n-2)}}\]

to get the (A.3) into the form appropriate to fitting:

\[\frac{\mathrm{d}^{2}\sigma^{c\bar{c}}}{2\pi p_{T}\mathrm{d}p_{T}\mathrm{d}y}= \frac{1}{2\pi}\frac{\mathrm{d}\sigma^{c\bar{c}}}{\mathrm{d}y}\frac{(n-1)(n-2) }{(nT+m_{0})[m_{0}(n-1)+nT]}\left(\frac{nT+\sqrt{p_{T}^{2}+m_{0}^{2}}}{nT+m_ {0}}\right)^{-n}\] (A.4)

