### B.1 General Relations

Consider the decay of a \(D^{*}\) of mass \(M\) in its CMS frame. Then its 4-momentum is P\({}^{*}\) = \((M,0,0,0)\). Denote the 4-momenta of the daughter particles in CMS by p\({}_{1}^{*}\) (for \(D^{0}\)) and p\({}_{2}^{*}\) (for \(\pi_{S}\)): p\({}_{1}^{*}\) = \((E_{1}^{*},{\bf p}_{1}^{*})\), p\({}_{2}^{*}\) = \((E_{2}^{*},{\bf p}_{2}^{*})\). The 4-momentum conservation requires P\({}^{*}\) = p\({}_{1}^{*}\) + p\({}_{2}^{*}\) and hence \({\bf p}_{2}^{*}\) = \(-{\bf p}_{1}^{*}\). We can therefore omit the subscript on the daughter particle momenta and hence energy conservation takes on the form \(E_{1}+E_{2}=\sqrt{m_{1}^{2}+p^{*2}}+\sqrt{m_{2}^{2}+p^{*2}}=M\). Solving this equation for \(p^{*}\) we get

\[p^{*}=\frac{1}{2M}\sqrt{[M^{2}+(m_{1}-m_{2})^{2}][M^{2}-(m_{1}-m_{2})^{2}]}=39.397\ {\rm MeV},\] (B.1)

where \(M=2010.28\) MeV, \(m_{1}=1864.84\) MeV, and \(m_{2}=139.57\) MeV.

