To find the shortest interval \([\alpha,\beta]\) which contains probability content \(\lambda=0.683\) (corresponds to \(\pm 1\sigma\) interval of a Normal distribution), the interval \(\beta-\alpha\) must be minimized, subject to the constraint

\[\int\limits_{\alpha}^{\beta}P(\varepsilon;k,n)\mathrm{d}\varepsilon=\lambda\]

A formal solution can be found using the method of Lagrange multipliers. The solution for \(\alpha\) and \(\beta\) is found by the simultaneous solutions of the nonlinear equations:

\[\mathcal{G}+\rho\alpha^{k}(1-\alpha)^{n-k} =0\] \[\mathcal{G}+\rho\beta^{k}(1-\beta)^{n-k} =0\] \[\mathcal{B}_{\beta}(k+1,n-k+1)-\mathcal{B}_{\alpha}(k+1,n-k+1) =\lambda\mathcal{G}\]

where \(\mathcal{G}=\dfrac{\Gamma(k+1)\Gamma(n-k+1)}{\Gamma(n+2)}\), \(\rho\) is a Lagrange multiplier introduced for the constraint, and \(\mathcal{B}_{x}(k+1,n-k+1)\) is the incomplete Beta function, defined by

\[\mathcal{B}_{x}(k+1,n-k+1)=\int\limits_{0}^{x}t^{k}(1-t)^{n-k}\mathrm{d}t.\]

The calculation of \(\lambda\) is implemented in the ROOT routine TGraphAsymmErrors::BayesDivide.

### 15 Treatment of Systematic Errors

This appendix is based on the article [81].

Suppose a yield is estimated from a data sample by two different methods, giving estimates \(Y_{1}\) and \(Y_{2}\), with statistical errors \(\sigma_{1}\) and \(\sigma_{2}\). It is required to find error on the difference

\[\Delta=Y_{1}-Y_{2} \tag{15}\]and thus to establish whether the estimates agree within one standard deviation. The error on the difference is given by

\[\sigma_{\Delta}^{2}=\sigma_{1}^{2}+\sigma_{2}^{2}-2\rho\sigma_{1}\sigma_{2}\] (C.8)

where \(\rho\) is the correlation between the two estimates. If two estimates are, at any rate to some extent, independent, they can be weighted and combined to give an improved estimate that is more efficient than either one separately. Let the joint estimator be

\[Y=wY_{1}+(1-w)Y_{2}\] (C.9)

where \(w\) is some chosen weight. Then the error on \(Y\) is given by

\[\sigma^{2}=w^{2}\sigma_{1}^{2}+(1-w)^{2}\sigma_{2}^{2}+2w(1-w)\rho\sigma_{1} \sigma_{2}.\] (C.10)

Setting the derivative with respect to \(w\) to zero to find the minimum of \(\sigma\) gives

\[w=\frac{\sigma_{2}^{2}-\rho\sigma_{1}\sigma_{2}}{\sigma_{1}^{2}+\sigma_{2}^{2 }-2\rho\sigma_{1}\sigma_{2}}\qquad 1-w=\frac{\sigma_{1}^{2}-\rho\sigma_{1} \sigma_{2}}{\sigma_{1}^{2}+\sigma_{2}^{2}-2\rho\sigma_{1}\sigma_{2}}\] (C.11)

and the resulting minimum error on \(Y\) is given by

\[\sigma=\frac{\sigma_{1}^{2}\sigma_{2}^{2}(1-\rho^{2})}{\sigma_{1}^{2}+\sigma _{2}^{2}-2\rho\sigma_{1}\sigma_{2}}\] (C.12)

(If the results are uncorrelated this gives the familiar form for the best combination of estimates with different errors).

However no estimator can be more efficient than the Minimum Variance Bound (MVB)

\[\sigma_{0}=\frac{1}{N\int\left(\frac{\mathrm{d}\ln P(x;Y)}{\mathrm{d}Y}\right) ^{2}P(x;Y)\mathrm{d}x}\] (C.13)

where \(P(x;Y)\) is the probability density function for the distribution being measured. Thus

\[\frac{\sigma_{1}^{2}\sigma_{2}^{2}(1-\rho^{2})}{\sigma_{1}^{2}+\sigma_{2}^{2 }-2\rho\sigma_{1}\sigma_{2}}\geq\sigma_{0}\] (C.14)which gives an expression for \(\rho\) which is satisfied only between the two roots of the quadratic

\[\rho=\frac{\sigma_{0}^{2}\pm\sqrt{(\sigma_{1}^{2}-\sigma_{0}^{2})(\sigma_{2}^{2}- \sigma_{0}^{2})}}{\sigma_{1}\sigma_{2}}\] (C.15)

The maximum value for \(\sigma_{\Delta}\) comes from the minimum value for \(\rho\), and vice versa

\[\begin{array}{l}\sigma_{\Delta}^{max}=\sqrt{(\sigma_{1}^{2}-\sigma_{0}^{2}) }+\sqrt{(\sigma_{2}^{2}-\sigma_{0}^{2})}\\ \sigma_{\Delta}^{min}=\left|\sqrt{(\sigma_{1}^{2}-\sigma_{0}^{2})}-\sqrt{( \sigma_{2}^{2}-\sigma_{0}^{2})}\right|\end{array}\] (C.16)

## Appendix C.3 Treatment of Correlated Errors

This appendix is based on the article [92].

Suppose \(n\) measurements \(x_{i}\). Let's define the covariance matrix \(\mathbb{V}\), whose terms are given by

\[\mathbb{V}_{ij}=\mathrm{E}\left[(x_{i}-\mu_{i})(x_{j}-\mu_{j})\right]=\mathrm{ E}[x_{i}x_{j}]-\mu_{i}\mu_{j}\]

where \(\mathrm{E}\) denotes expected value and \(\mu\) mean value (\(\mu\equiv\mathrm{E}(x)\)). The diagonal members of \(\mathbb{V}\) are the variances, e.g., \(\mathbb{V}_{ii}=\sigma_{i}^{2}\). The correlation coefficient \(r_{ij}\) is given by

\[r_{ij}=\frac{\mathbb{V}_{ij}}{\sigma_{i}\sigma_{j}}.\]

The best linear estimator accounting for all measurements can be defined generally as

\[\overline{x}\equiv\sum_{i}w_{i}x_{i}\] (C.17)

where the weights \(w_{i}\) must be found. Assuming that the \(x_{i}\) each provide an unbiased estimate and requiring that the estimator \(\overline{x}\) be unbiased gives the condition \(\sum_{i}w_{i}=1\). Writing \(\overline{x}-\mathrm{E}(\overline{x})=\sum_{i}w_{i}(x_{i}-\mathrm{E}(x_{i}))\) and using the definition of the standard deviation, one can obtain the relation for the standard deviation of \(\overline{x}\):

\[\sigma_{\overline{x}}^{2}=\sum_{ij}w_{i}w_{j}\mathbb{V}_{ij}\] (C.18)To minimize \(\sigma_{\overline{x}}^{2}\) subject to condition \(\sum_{i}w_{i}=1\), the Lagrange multiplier technique can be used. Writing

\[\sigma_{\overline{x}}^{2}=\sum_{ij}w_{i}w_{j}\mathbb{V}_{ij}+\lambda\left(\sum_ {i}w_{i}-1\right)\] (C.19)

and setting the derivative of \(\sigma_{\overline{x}}^{2}\) with respect to the \(w_{i}\) and \(\lambda\) to zero, one gets the solution1

Footnote 1: Actually, \(\mathbb{V}^{-1}\) does not have to exist for the weights to be determined. Such singular covariance matrices arise when only a fully correlated error is present. The simplest way to deal with this situation numerically is to add a small term \(\epsilon\) to the diagonal elements of \(\mathbb{V}\), calculate \(\mathbb{V}^{-1}\), and neglect \(\epsilon\) afterwards.

\[w_{i}=\frac{\sum_{k}\mathbb{V}_{ik}^{-1}}{\sum_{jk}\mathbb{V}_{jk}^{-1}}\] (C.20)

